CAT = "s65FixedIncome"; SEG = "FIX"
Q = [
('A client wants an insured place to park cash she may need any day. The best fit is:', 'A bank demand deposit', ['A long-term bank CD', 'A 30-year Treasury bond', 'A corporate note'], 'A demand deposit (checking account) is FDIC insured within limits and can be withdrawn at any time. A CD ties the money up until maturity or charges a penalty.'),
('Which money market instrument is an unsecured short-term note issued by a corporation?', 'Commercial paper', ['A Treasury bill', 'A negotiable CD', 'A repurchase agreement'], 'Commercial paper is unsecured corporate debt, usually maturing in a few months. Treasury bills are federal government obligations.'),
('A client in a state with a high income tax wants to reduce state tax on her interest income. Which holding helps most?', 'Treasury notes', ['Corporate bonds', 'Bank CDs', 'Foreign government bonds'], 'Interest on Treasury securities is taxed federally but exempt from state and local income tax. Corporate bond and CD interest is taxed by both.'),
('A Treasury Inflation-Protected Security with $1,000 principal pays a 2% coupon. CPI rises 4% over the year. The principal becomes about:', '$1,040', ['$1,020', '$1,060', '$1,000'], 'TIPS principal is indexed to the CPI, so it rises 4% to about $1,040, and the 2% coupon is paid on the adjusted principal.'),
('In a year of deflation, the principal of a Treasury Inflation-Protected Security:', 'Is adjusted down', ['Stays at its last level', 'Is adjusted up', 'Converts to a T-bill'], 'TIPS principal falls with the CPI in deflation, which lowers the interest paid. At maturity the investor still receives the greater of adjusted or original principal.'),
('A bond backed by a pool of auto loans is best described as:', 'An asset-backed security', ['A debenture', 'A general obligation bond', 'A mortgage bond'], 'Asset-backed securities are paid from a pool of receivables such as auto loans or credit card balances. A mortgage bond is secured by real property of the issuer.'),
('A city bond is repaid only from water and sewer fees. If those fees fall short, bondholders:', 'Have no claim on city taxes', ['Can force a property tax increase', 'Are paid from the general fund', 'Are paid by the state'], "A revenue bond is backed only by the revenue of its project. The city's taxing power stands behind general obligation bonds, not revenue bonds."),
("An insured municipal bond's rating mainly reflects:", "The insurer's credit", ["The issuer's tax base alone", "The bond's coupon rate", "The state's credit rating"], "Bond insurance guarantees principal and interest, so the rating largely follows the insurer's credit. If the insurer is downgraded, the bond can be too."),
('An investor in the 35% federal bracket is choosing between munis and taxable bonds. A 4.55% municipal bond has a taxable-equivalent yield of:', '7.0%', ['6.14%', '2.96%', '5.55%'], 'Taxable-equivalent yield = municipal yield / (1 - tax rate) = 4.55% / 0.65 = 7.0%. Multiplying by 1.35 (6.14%) understates it.'),
("A U.S. investor buys bonds issued by a foreign corporation in that country's currency. Compared with a similar U.S. bond, the added risk is:", 'Currency risk', ['Call risk', 'Inflation risk', 'Prepayment risk'], 'Payments in a foreign currency are worth more or fewer dollars as exchange rates move. Foreign bonds can also carry political and liquidity risk.'),
('A bond rated A by one agency is downgraded to BB. The bond has moved:', 'From investment grade to junk', ['Within investment grade', 'From junk to investment grade', 'Into default'], 'BBB (Baa) is the lowest investment-grade rating. BB and below are speculative, or high-yield, bonds.'),
('A 10-year bond yields 4.9% and a Treasury of the same maturity yields 4.1%. The credit spread is:', '80 basis points', ['8 basis points', '490 basis points', '90 basis points'], '4.9% - 4.1% = 0.80 percentage points, or 80 basis points (one basis point is 0.01%).'),
('A 12-year zero-coupon bond has a Macaulay duration of:', '12 years', ['Less than 12 years', 'About 6 years', 'More than 12 years'], 'A zero pays everything at maturity, so its duration equals its maturity. Any coupon bond of the same maturity has a shorter duration.'),
('A bond with a duration of 6 sees market rates rise 1%. Its price will fall by about:', '6%', ['1%', '0.6%', '16%'], 'Duration estimates the percent price change for a 1% change in yield: 6 x 1% = about 6%.'),
("A bond's quoted yield to maturity assumes that the investor:", 'Reinvests coupons at that yield', ['Spends every coupon', 'Sells before maturity', 'Reinvests at the coupon rate'], 'YTM is an internal rate of return, so it assumes each coupon is reinvested at the same yield and the bond is held to maturity. Lower reinvestment rates mean a lower realized return.'),
('A callable bond trades at a premium. When quoting its yield, a firm should use:', 'The lower of YTM and YTC', ['The higher of YTM and YTC', 'The coupon rate', 'The current yield'], 'Customers must be given the worst-case yield. For a premium callable bond, an early call is the worst case, so yield to call is usually the lower figure.'),
('Why does a callable bond usually pay a higher yield than a similar non-callable bond?', 'Investors bear call risk', ['It has more collateral', 'It ranks above other debt', 'Its interest is tax-free'], 'The issuer will call the bond when rates fall, forcing reinvestment at lower yields. Investors want extra yield to accept that risk.'),
("A convertible bond with $1,000 par converts into 32 shares. The stock trades at $34. The bond's conversion value is:", '$1,088', ['$1,000', '$1,066', '$1,034'], 'Conversion value = shares x stock price = 32 x $34 = $1,088. Parity price is $1,000 / 32 = $31.25, below the current $34.'),
('A zero-coupon bond held in a taxable account creates which tax issue?', 'Tax due on interest not received', ['No tax until it is sold', 'Interest taxed only when it matures', 'Losses that cannot be deducted'], 'The discount on a zero accretes each year and is taxed as interest even though no cash is paid, often called phantom income. Zeros suit IRAs for that reason.'),
("A bond's price is the present value of its future cash flows. If the discount rate used rises, the price:", 'Falls', ['Rises', 'Stays the same', 'Equals par'], 'Discounting the same coupons and principal at a higher rate gives a smaller present value, which is why bond prices fall when yields rise.'),
]
